1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,000
A complete static reverse-engineering of one Vietnamese payment app — every file, measured.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,500
The question: what does a regulated wallet actually contain, and where does the plaintext end?

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:16,000
Three hypotheses: four trust domains, defence in depth native, integrity as the weak link.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,800
Nothing here touches ZaloPay infrastructure. Everything is derived from the archive on disk.

5
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,500
Act I. The ZIP is not the app: an XAPK wraps a base APK and four configuration splits.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,500
Five APKs, 6,464 entries, 186 MiB uncompressed. The base carries code; the ABI split carries 24 MiB of C++.

7
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:39,500
Bytecode is 61.8 MiB of the payload, native libraries 54.8 MiB, and ML models a further 14.5 MiB.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,500
Multidex is forced by the format: three DEX files sit within 5,600 references of the 65,536 method limit.

9
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:55,000
Forensics: 4,249 payloads are not 4-byte aligned. This bundle was re-packed, not served by Play.

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,500
No v1 JAR signature in the base APK — verification can only rest on the v2 signing block.

11
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:05,500
Act II. A custom DEX reader decodes every class, method and string constant.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
60,671 classes, 351,248 method references, 374,276 string constants across 19 DEX files.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,500
A DEX is index tables first, code payload last — 2.2 MiB of indexes in the largest file.

14
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Three files are at 91% of the method-id ceiling: packaging forces multidex, not architecture.

15
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,500
R8 shortens 28.5% of packages, yet 56 third-party SDKs remain identifiable from string evidence.

16
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,500
URL templates, protobuf schemas and JNI symbols survive obfuscation and identify the whole stack.

17
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:49,500
Act III. Twenty-eight ELF objects, 54.8 MiB, 27 in the ABI split plus one in assets.

18
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,500
Risk scoring, liveness, encrypted storage and crypto are all native; the JVM only orchestrates.

19
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Hardening: RELRO 26/27, BIND_NOW 27/27, NX 27/27, PIE 26/27, stack canaries 25/27.

20
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,500
Seven libraries carry randomised filenames; each registers its JNI symbols at load time.

21
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Crypto inventory: AES-GCM, RSA-OAEP, HMAC, PBKDF2, X25519, Ed25519 — plus a full post-quantum provider.

22
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:29,500
Post-quantum hits are string-level evidence only: the algorithms are linked, not proven to protect traffic.

23
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,500
Act IV. 168 hosts, 45 first-party: ZaloPay owns the wallet, not the whole pipe.

24
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:41,000
290 REST paths survive as strings — the protocol surface stays legible even when classes do not.

25
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,500
373 mini-apps ship as configuration in one 883 KiB JSON, executed by the Alibaba mPaaS runtime.

26
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:58,000
Endpoint names describe protocol shape: per-user salt, server public key, PIN pre-check, OTP sessions.

27
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:06,500
49 permissions, 14 dangerous-class: contacts, camera, location, phone state, audio.

28
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,500
But names reveal no envelope format, no key exchange and no PIN block. That is where this study stops.

29
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:17,500
Act V. The signer certificate is real, self-signed in 2016, valid until 2041.

30
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:25,000
A self-signed 25-year certificate is normal for an app that has shipped since 2016.

31
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:33,500
But identity is not integrity: the digest must also match the code, and here it does not.

32
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:41,500
The signing block declares 1,383 bytes for a pair whose payload is 1,387 — a parser desynchronises.

33
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:49,500
A duplicate, unaligned pair re-embeds the signed data. Neither parse nor signature survives.

34
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,500
Verdict: structurally parsed, cryptographically unproven. Unverified is a finding, not a failure.

35
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:59,500
Act VI. Nine findings, one reproducible pipeline.

36
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Structure, code mass, obfuscation, vendor gravity, native secrets, ML, ecosystem, privacy, integrity.

37
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:15,500
Method: hash, decode, tag evidence, verify in layers, then render the film from the emitted JSON.

38
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,500
Every figure regenerates from research/data — the pipeline and the film ship together.

39
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:31,500
Cite the atlas, not the screenshots: the archive hash pins exactly what was analysed.

40
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,800
Static analysis only. No secret, key, PIN or customer record is reproduced in this work.
